   "tarih": "2026-10-30",
   "baslik": "Haunting the Cepheus Flare",
   "tur": "image",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-30-haunting-the-cepheus-flare/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: Spooky shapes seem to haunt this dusty expanse, drifting through the night in the royal constellation Cepheus. Of course, the shapes are cosmic dust clouds visible in dimly reflected starlight.",
   "kredi": "Mirko Tondinelli",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
  },
  {
   "tarih": "2026-10-29",
   "baslik": "The Horns of Venus",
   "tur": "image",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-29-october-2026-the-horns-of-venus/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: That's no moon. But you could be forgiven for thinking it was a crescent Moon in the sky. It is a lesser known fact that Venus also has phases. (What about other planets?) The featured image shows Venus seen almost directly between the Sun and the Earth.",
   "kredi": "Thierry Legault",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
  },
  {
   "tarih": "2026-10-28",
   "baslik": "The Mutual Occultation of Ganymede and Io",
   "tur": "video",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-28-the-mutual-occultation-of-ganymede-and-io/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: Jupiter is one of the least lonely planets in the Solar System, at least when it comes to natural satellites. 115 moons have been discovered orbiting Jupiter so far! (Saturn has the most with 293, but who’s counting.) Ganymede and Io, two of Jupiter’s Galilean moons, met in the sky on October 20th, 2026.",
   "kredi": "Michael Wong",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
  },
  {
   "tarih": "2026-10-27",
   "baslik": "Noctilucent Clouds over Sweden",
   "tur": "image",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-27-noctilucent-clouds-over-sweden/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: Sometimes it's night on the ground but day in the air. As the Earth rotates to eclipse the Sun, sunset rises up from the ground. Therefore, at sunset on the ground, sunlight still shines on clouds above.",
   "kredi": "V. Höfer, D. Večerník &amp; B. Večerníková",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
  },
  {
   "tarih": "2026-10-26",
   "baslik": "M42: The Great Nebula in Orion",
   "tur": "image",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod/2026-october-26-m42-the-great-nebula-in-orion/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: The Great Nebula in Orion, an immense, nearby starbirth region, is one of the most famous of all astronomical nebulas. Here, glowing gas surrounds hot young stars at the edge of an immense interstellar molecular cloud only 1500 light-years away.",
   "kredi": "Roberto Palaia",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
  },
  {
   "tarih": "2026-10-25",
   "baslik": "Dark Matter in a Simulated Universe",
   "tur": "image",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-25-dark-matter-in-a-simulated-universe/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: Is our universe haunted? It might look that way on this dark matter map. The gravity of unseen dark matter is the leading explanation for why galaxies rotate so fast, why galaxies orbit clusters so fast, why gravitational lenses so strongly deflect light, and how visible matter is distributed.",
   "kredi": "Tom Abel &amp; Ralf Kaehler (KIPAC , SLAC), AMNH",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
  },
  {
   "tarih": "2026-10-24",
   "baslik": "Rosetta's Selfie",
   "tur": "image",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-24-rosettas-selfie/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: This Rosetta spacecraft selfie was snapped on October 7th, 2014. At the time the comet-chasing spacecraft was about 472 million kilometers from planet Earth, but only 16 kilometers from the surface of comet 67P/Churyumov-Gerasimenko.",
   "kredi": "ESA/Rosetta/Philae/CIVA",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
  },
  {
   "tarih": "2026-10-23",
   "baslik": "Andromeda in Southern Spring",
   "tur": "image",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-23-andromeda-in-southern-spring/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: Looking toward the north from Mullion, New South Wales, Australia, the Andromeda Galaxy never gets more than a few degrees above the horizon. During the southern hemisphere spring, Andromeda is highest in the sky around midnight though.",
   "kredi": "Lucy Yunxi Hu",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
  },
  {
   "tarih": "2026-10-22",
   "baslik": "The First Green Star, WD J1330+6435",
   "tur": "image",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-22-the-first-green-star-wd-j13306435/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: Star light, star bright: is there a green star out tonight? Other than the Sun, all stars are so far from Earth that they appear as points in the sky. They are mostly white, but sometimes noticeably red or blue. This color typically relates to the temperature: blue stars are hotter than red stars.",
   "kredi": "Mark A. Garlick/University of Warwick",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
  },
  {
   "tarih": "2026-10-21",
   "baslik": "New Ghosts Revealed in Cassiopeia",
   "tur": "image",
   "baglanti": "https://science.nasa.gov/image-article/apod-2026-october-21-new-ghosts-revealed-in-cassiopeia/",
   "ozet_en": "Explanation: Jinkies, look at all those ghosts! It took 100 hours of observation and a special oxygen filter for today’s image to capture three faint nebulas hidden in the Cassiopeia constellation. IC 59 and IC 63, the original Ghosts of Cassiopeia, haunt the region near multi-star system γ Cassiopeia.",
   "kredi": "Louis Leroux-Géré, Thibault Rouillée, Vincent Martin, Cédric de Decker (Starfront Observatories)",
   "nasa_kaynakli": false
  }
